1. What are wrapper classes in java?
Wrapper classes are used to covert primitive data type into objects
java has primtive data types like int, char, double
but collections work only objects
so java provide wrapper classes to wrap primtive into objects.
int -> integer
float-> float etc.
int a = 10;
        // converting primitive to object
        Integer obj = Integer.valueOf(a);
        System.out.println(obj);

        // converting object to primitive
        int b = obj.intValue();
        System.out.println(b);

2.Why do we need Wrapper Classes?
Required in Collections (Collections store objects only)
Used in APIs and frameworks
Provide utility methods
List all Wrapper Classes
| Primitive | Wrapper   |
| ---------     | --------- |
| int              | Integer   |
| char          | Character |
| double     | Double    |
| float          | Float     |
| boolean   | Boolean   |
| long           | Long      |
| short          | Short     |
| byte      | Byte      |
4.What is Boxing and Unboxing?
Boxing → primitive → object
Unboxing → object → primitive
Example Autoboxing
int a = 10;
Integer obj = a;  // automatic conversion

Example unboxing
Integer obj = 20;
int a = obj;  // automatic conversion

5.What is Auto-boxing and Auto-unboxing?
Autoboxing is automatic conversion of primitive to wrapper object, and unboxing is conversion of wrapper object to primitive.
Example
int a = 10;
Integer obj = a;  // autoboxing

Example
Integer obj = Integer.valueOf(a);

